<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Smilin' Through</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in film (Jeanette MacDonald)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Clockwork Orange (Stanley Kubrick)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hope and Glory (John Boorman)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Football chants (Wolverhampton Wanderers, West Bromwich Albion, Huddersfield Town, Chelsea and others)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 (Elgar)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1901</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (its trio)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Coronation Ode (Elgar, words by A. C. Benson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1902</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted from (its final section)</span></li><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1745</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first five notes similar</span></li></ul></section>
